# API

Everything is exported from `src/index.mjs`. The names and shapes on this page are a contract with the services that use walq.

## openQueue(dir, options)

| Option | Default | Meaning |
|---|---|---|
| `leaseMs` | 30 000 | Default lease length |
| `lockTimeoutMs` | 10 000 | How long a call waits for the lock before `LockTimeoutError` |
| `compactAfterBytes` | 4 MiB | Compact automatically past this size. `0` turns it off |
| `retainCompletedMs` | 24 h | How long finished jobs stay in the log after they finish |
| `idempotencyTtlMs` | 7 d | How long a key is remembered, from when the job was enqueued |
| `maxAttempts` | 5 | Default attempts per job |
| `backoff` | `{ baseMs: 1000, factor: 2, maxMs: 60000, jitter: 0 }` | Retry schedule |
| `clock` | system clock | Anything with `now()`. Tests use `ManualClock` |

## Queue

**`enqueue(type, payload, { key, delayMs, maxAttempts, priority })`** returns `{ id, status, deduped }`. With a remembered `key` it also returns the existing job's `result` and `deduped: true`, and creates nothing.

**`enqueueMany([{ type, payload, options }])`** adds a batch under one lock and one sync. A crash can leave a prefix of the batch behind.

**`claim(worker, { leaseMs, types })`** returns `{ id, type, payload, attempt, token, leaseUntil }` or `null`. Among due jobs, higher `priority` goes first, then the job due longest, then the oldest. `token` identifies this attempt.

**`heartbeat(id, token, { leaseMs })`** returns `{ leaseUntil }`. Throws `StaleLeaseError` if the lease is no longer yours.

**`complete(id, token, result)`** records the result. Throws `StaleLeaseError` if the lease is no longer yours. RFC 007 changes what this returns.

**`fail(id, token, error)`** returns `{ id, status, retryAt }`. The job goes back to `ready` with a backoff, or to `dead` when attempts run out.

**`reapExpired()`** returns jobs whose lease had run out to the queue, and returns `[{ id, status, retryAt }]`.

**`cancel(id, { reason })`** stops a job that is `ready` or `leased`. Returns `{ id, status, changed }`. A worker holding the job loses its lease.

**`requeueDead(id)`** gives a dead job a new set of attempts, runnable now. Returns `{ id, status }`. Throws `InvalidTransitionError` if the job is not dead.

**`getJob(id)`** returns a copy of the job or `null`. **`listJobs()`** returns copies of all jobs in the log. **`stats()`** returns counts by status, plus `total`, `generation`, `logBytes`, `tornTailsDropped` and `lastCompactionError`. **`metrics()`** is described in [operations.md](operations.md).

**`compact()`** rewrites the log now and returns `{ generation, bytesBefore, bytesAfter, jobs }`. If it throws, the queue is still usable and nothing is lost.

**`close()`** marks this instance closed.

## Job fields

`id`, `type`, `payload`, `key`, `priority`, `status` (`ready`, `leased`, `done`, `dead`, `cancelled`), `attempts`, `fence` (the current token), `maxAttempts`, `availableAt`, `lease` (`{ worker, fence, until }` or null), `result`, `lastError`, `enqueuedAt`, `completedAt`, `seq`.

## WorkerPool

```js
new WorkerPool({ dir, handlers, size, leaseMs, heartbeatMs, pollMs, reapMs, types, env, queue, maxRespawns })
```

`handlers` is the path of a handlers module. `queue` is the options object for `openQueue` in the workers and the pool, so it must be JSON-serialisable. `env` adds environment variables for the workers. `types` limits which job types the pool's workers take.

- `start()` starts the workers.
- `drain({ timeoutMs })` resolves when no job is `ready` or `leased`, and rejects when that takes too long.
- `stop()` stops the workers and waits for them to go.

The pool is an `EventEmitter`. `message` carries worker events (`kind` is one of `ready`, `job-started`, `job-completed`, `job-failed`, `job-fenced`, `worker-error`), `exit` fires when a worker exits, and `stderr` and `error-event` report problems.

## Errors

`WalqError` is the base class. `CorruptLogError` has an `offset`. Also `StaleLeaseError`, `UnknownJobError`, `LockTimeoutError`, `InvalidTransitionError`, `ConfigError`, `QueueClosedError`.

## Other exports

`computeBackoff(attempts, policy, salt)`, `ManualClock`, `SystemClock`, `JobRunner`, `inspectLog(dir)`, `describeRecord(record)`, `onFailpoint`, `clearFailpoints`, `hit`.
# Operations

## Files

A queue directory holds `wal.log` and `queue.lock`. Temp files named `wal.log.*` can appear during compaction. Don't edit any of these by hand while the queue is in use.

## Logging

Silent by default. Set `WALQ_LOG=info` (or `debug`, `warn`, `error`) for JSON lines on stderr.

## metrics()

| Field | Meaning |
|---|---|
| `byType` | Job counts by type and status |
| `dueNow` | Ready jobs whose time has come |
| `delayed` | Ready jobs waiting for their time |
| `retriedJobs` | Jobs on their second attempt or later |
| `oldestReadyAgeMs` | How long the longest-waiting due job has waited |
| `nextLeaseExpiryMs` | Time until the next lease deadline, or null |
| `activeWorkers` | Distinct workers holding leases |
| `idempotencyKeys` | Keys currently remembered |
| `jobsEverEnqueued` | Total since the queue was created |
| `generation`, `logBytes` | The log |
| `tornTailsDropped` | Incomplete tails this process has dealt with |
| `lastCompactionError` | Why the last automatic compaction failed, or null |

## Tools

```
node src/cli.mjs inspect <dir>        every record in the log
node src/cli.mjs stats <dir>          job counts
node src/cli.mjs metrics <dir>        the table above
node src/cli.mjs compact <dir>        compact now
node src/cli.mjs tail <dir>           follow the log as it grows
node src/cli.mjs requeue <dir> <id>   give a dead job new attempts
node src/cli.mjs cancel <dir> <id>    stop a job
```

`inspect` takes no lock and changes nothing, so it's safe at any time. `tail` keeps the log open for as long as it runs.

## Sizing

Each write takes the lock and syncs, so throughput follows the disk's sync latency. The lock is shared by all processes on a queue, so a handful of workers per queue is the expected size. Run more queues rather than more workers.

## Upgrades

The log format is stable. Stop the workers, deploy, and start them again. Older logs open without any conversion.
# Changelog

## 0.4.2

- Throughput work for busy queues: less log growth and fewer syncs in the common paths.
- Lower memory use when a process catches up.

## 0.4.1

- `cancel()` and `requeueDead()`.
- Job priorities.
- `metrics()` and the `metrics` command.

## 0.4.0

- Smaller snapshots.
- Worker pools replace dead workers automatically.
- Failpoints for crash testing.

## 0.3.0

- Idempotency keys.
- Automatic compaction past `compactAfterBytes`.

## 0.2.0

- Multi-process access through a shared lock. Workers became child processes.

## 0.1.0

- First version: one process, one log.
// On-disk encoding of the log. The layout is specified in docs/wal-format.md and
// is a compatibility contract: logs already in production must stay readable.

import { crc32 } from './crc32.mjs';
import { CorruptLogError } from './errors.mjs';

export const FORMAT_VERSION = 1;
export const HEADER_SIZE = 20;
export const FRAME_OVERHEAD = 10;

const FILE_MAGIC = Buffer.from('WALQ', 'latin1');
const FRAME_MAGIC = 0xfbad;
const MAX_PAYLOAD = 64 * 1024 * 1024;

export function encodeHeader(generation) {
  const header = Buffer.alloc(HEADER_SIZE);
  FILE_MAGIC.copy(header, 0);
  header.writeUInt16LE(FORMAT_VERSION, 4);
  header.writeUInt32LE(generation, 8);
  header.writeUInt32LE(crc32(header.subarray(0, 16)), 16);
  return header;
}

export function decodeHeader(bytes, file = 'log') {
  if (bytes.length < HEADER_SIZE) {
    throw new CorruptLogError(`${file}: header is missing or short (${bytes.length} bytes)`, { offset: 0 });
  }
  if (!bytes.subarray(0, 4).equals(FILE_MAGIC)) {
    throw new CorruptLogError(`${file}: not a walq log`, { offset: 0 });
  }
  if (bytes.readUInt32LE(16) !== crc32(bytes.subarray(0, 16))) {
    throw new CorruptLogError(`${file}: header checksum mismatch`, { offset: 0 });
  }
  const version = bytes.readUInt16LE(4);
  if (version !== FORMAT_VERSION) {
    throw new CorruptLogError(`${file}: unsupported format version ${version}`, { offset: 0 });
  }
  return { version, generation: bytes.readUInt32LE(8) };
}

export function encodeFrame(record) {
  const payload = Buffer.from(JSON.stringify(record), 'utf8');
  const frame = Buffer.allocUnsafe(FRAME_OVERHEAD + payload.length);
  frame.writeUInt16LE(FRAME_MAGIC, 0);
  frame.writeUInt32LE(payload.length, 2);
  frame.writeUInt32LE(crc32(payload, crc32(frame.subarray(2, 6))), 6);
  payload.copy(frame, FRAME_OVERHEAD);
  return frame;
}

function decodeFrameAt(bytes, pos) {
  const remaining = bytes.length - pos;
  if (remaining < FRAME_OVERHEAD) return { ok: false, reason: 'partial' };
  if (bytes.readUInt16LE(pos) !== FRAME_MAGIC) return { ok: false, reason: 'bad-magic' };
  const length = bytes.readUInt32LE(pos + 2);
  if (length > MAX_PAYLOAD) return { ok: false, reason: 'bad-length' };
  if (remaining < FRAME_OVERHEAD + length) return { ok: false, reason: 'partial' };
  const start = pos + FRAME_OVERHEAD;
  const payload = bytes.subarray(start, start + length);
  const expected = bytes.readUInt32LE(pos + 6);
  if (expected !== crc32(payload, crc32(bytes.subarray(pos + 2, pos + 6)))) {
    return { ok: false, reason: 'bad-checksum' };
  }
  try {
    return { ok: true, record: JSON.parse(payload.toString('utf8')), next: start + length };
  } catch {
    return { ok: false, reason: 'bad-payload' };
  }
}

/**
 * Walks frames starting at the beginning of `bytes`, which sit at `baseOffset`
 * in the file. Returns the decoded frames, the file offset just after the last
 * good one, and `tail` when the bytes after that are not a complete frame.
 */
export function scanFrames(bytes, baseOffset = 0) {
  const frames = [];
  let pos = 0;
  let tail = null;
  while (pos < bytes.length) {
    const decoded = decodeFrameAt(bytes, pos);
    if (!decoded.ok) {
      if (decoded.reason !== 'partial') {
        throw new CorruptLogError(
          `corrupt record at offset ${baseOffset + pos} (${decoded.reason})`,
          { offset: baseOffset + pos, reason: decoded.reason },
        );
      }
      tail = { offset: baseOffset + pos, reason: decoded.reason, bytes: bytes.length - pos };
      break;
    }
    frames.push({ offset: baseOffset + pos, size: decoded.next - pos, record: decoded.record });
    pos = decoded.next;
  }
  return { frames, end: baseOffset + pos, tail };
}
// The log file itself: a header, then checksummed frames, written only by the
// process holding the queue lock.

import path from 'node:path';
import { decodeHeader, encodeFrame, encodeHeader, HEADER_SIZE, scanFrames } from './frame.mjs';
import { hit, isArmed } from './failpoints.mjs';

// Bounds memory when a process has fallen far behind. The next catch-up reads on
// from wherever this one stopped.
const SCAN_CHUNK = 1024 * 1024;

export class Wal {
  constructor(file, ops) {
    this.file = file;
    this.ops = ops;
  }

  exists() {
    return this.ops.exists(this.file);
  }

  /** Creates an empty log. The header appears atomically or not at all. */
  create(generation = 1) {
    const staging = `${this.file}.init`;
    this.ops.create(staging);
    this.ops.writeAt(staging, encodeHeader(generation), 0);
    this.ops.rename(staging, this.file);
    hit('wal.after-create', {});
    this.ops.sync(this.file);
    this.ops.syncDir(path.dirname(this.file));
  }

  readHeader() {
    return decodeHeader(this.ops.read(this.file, 0, HEADER_SIZE), this.file);
  }

  /** Decodes everything from `offset` to the end of the file. */
  scanFrom(offset) {
    return scanFrames(this.ops.read(this.file, offset, SCAN_CHUNK), offset);
  }

  truncate(length) {
    this.ops.truncate(this.file, length);
  }

  /** Writes records at the end of the file. Returns the new end offset. */
  append(records, { sync = true } = {}) {
    const type = records[0]?.t;
    const bytes = Buffer.concat(records.map(encodeFrame));
    const at = this.ops.size(this.file);
    hit('wal.before-append', { type });
    if (isArmed('wal.mid-append')) {
      const half = bytes.length >> 1;
      this.ops.writeAt(this.file, bytes.subarray(0, half), at);
      hit('wal.mid-append', { type });
      this.ops.writeAt(this.file, bytes.subarray(half), at + half);
    } else {
      this.ops.writeAt(this.file, bytes, at);
    }
    hit('wal.after-write', { type });
    if (sync) this.ops.sync(this.file);
    hit('wal.after-fsync', { type });
    return at + bytes.length;
  }
}
// All file access for the log goes through a FileOps object so that it can be
// swapped for the power-loss model described in docs/crash-testing.md.
//
// Operations are path based, so no handle stays open between calls.

import fs from 'node:fs';
import path from 'node:path';

const NO_DIR_SYNC = new Set(['EPERM', 'EISDIR', 'EINVAL', 'EBADF', 'EACCES']);

class RealFileOps {
  exists(file) {
    return fs.existsSync(file);
  }

  size(file) {
    return fs.statSync(file).size;
  }

  /** Reads from `offset` to `offset + length`, or to end of file. */
  read(file, offset = 0, length = Infinity) {
    const fd = fs.openSync(file, 'r');
    try {
      const available = Math.max(0, fs.fstatSync(fd).size - offset);
      const buffer = Buffer.allocUnsafe(Math.min(available, length));
      let filled = 0;
      while (filled < buffer.length) {
        const n = fs.readSync(fd, buffer, filled, buffer.length - filled, offset + filled);
        if (n === 0) break;
        filled += n;
      }
      return buffer.subarray(0, filled);
    } finally {
      fs.closeSync(fd);
    }
  }

  create(file, { exclusive = false } = {}) {
    fs.closeSync(fs.openSync(file, exclusive ? 'wx' : 'w'));
  }

  writeAt(file, bytes, position) {
    const fd = fs.openSync(file, 'r+');
    try {
      let written = 0;
      while (written < bytes.length) {
        written += fs.writeSync(fd, bytes, written, bytes.length - written, position + written);
      }
    } finally {
      fs.closeSync(fd);
    }
  }

  sync(file) {
    const fd = fs.openSync(file, 'r+');
    try {
      fs.fsyncSync(fd);
    } finally {
      fs.closeSync(fd);
    }
  }

  truncate(file, length) {
    const fd = fs.openSync(file, 'r+');
    try {
      fs.ftruncateSync(fd, length);
      fs.fsyncSync(fd);
    } finally {
      fs.closeSync(fd);
    }
  }

  rename(from, to) {
    fs.renameSync(from, to);
  }

  unlink(file) {
    fs.rmSync(file, { force: true });
  }

  /** Not every platform can sync a directory; those that cannot refuse with an error. */
  syncDir(dir) {
    let fd;
    try {
      fd = fs.openSync(dir, 'r');
      fs.fsyncSync(fd);
    } catch (err) {
      if (!NO_DIR_SYNC.has(err.code)) throw err;
    } finally {
      if (fd !== undefined) fs.closeSync(fd);
    }
  }
}

// Keeps written bytes in this process until sync(), like a page cache. A process
// killed at a failpoint therefore loses them, as a machine losing power would.
class PowerLossFileOps extends RealFileOps {
  constructor() {
    super();
    this.pending = new Map();
  }

  size(file) {
    let size = super.size(file);
    for (const { position, bytes } of this.pending.get(file) ?? []) {
      size = Math.max(size, position + bytes.length);
    }
    return size;
  }

  read(file, offset = 0, length = Infinity) {
    const writes = this.pending.get(file);
    if (!writes?.length) return super.read(file, offset, length);
    const image = Buffer.alloc(this.size(file));
    super.read(file).copy(image);
    for (const { position, bytes } of writes) bytes.copy(image, position);
    return image.subarray(offset, Math.min(image.length, offset + length));
  }

  create(file, options) {
    super.create(file, options);
    this.pending.delete(file);
  }

  writeAt(file, bytes, position) {
    if (!this.pending.has(file)) this.pending.set(file, []);
    this.pending.get(file).push({ position, bytes: Buffer.from(bytes) });
  }

  sync(file) {
    for (const { position, bytes } of this.pending.get(file) ?? []) {
      super.writeAt(file, bytes, position);
    }
    this.pending.delete(file);
    super.sync(file);
  }

  truncate(file, length) {
    super.truncate(file, length);
    const kept = (this.pending.get(file) ?? []).filter((w) => w.position + w.bytes.length <= length);
    this.pending.set(file, kept);
  }

  rename(from, to) {
    super.rename(from, to);
    const moving = this.pending.get(from);
    this.pending.delete(from);
    if (moving) this.pending.set(to, moving);
    else this.pending.delete(to);
  }

  unlink(file) {
    super.unlink(file);
    this.pending.delete(file);
  }
}

export function createFileOps() {
  return process.env.WALQ_SIMULATE_POWER_LOSS === '1' ? new PowerLossFileOps() : new RealFileOps();
}

export function ensureDir(dir) {
  fs.mkdirSync(path.resolve(dir), { recursive: true });
}
// Cross-process mutex. The lock is an exclusive-share handle on queue.lock, so
// the operating system drops it the moment the holder dies, however it dies.
// There is no stale-lock cleanup to get wrong.

import fs from 'node:fs';
import { sleepSync } from './clock.mjs';
import { LockTimeoutError } from './errors.mjs';

// libuv's UV_FS_O_EXLOCK: open with no sharing on Windows, O_EXLOCK on macOS.
const O_EXLOCK = 0x10000000;
const OPEN_FLAGS = fs.constants.O_CREAT | fs.constants.O_RDWR | O_EXLOCK;
const BUSY = new Set(['EBUSY', 'EPERM', 'EACCES', 'EAGAIN', 'EWOULDBLOCK']);

if (process.platform !== 'win32' && process.platform !== 'darwin') {
  throw new Error('walq needs exclusive-open locks, which this platform does not provide');
}

/** Blocks until the lock is held and returns the function that releases it. */
export function acquireLock(lockPath, { timeoutMs }) {
  const deadline = performance.now() + timeoutMs;
  let wait = 0.5;
  for (;;) {
    try {
      const fd = fs.openSync(lockPath, OPEN_FLAGS);
      return () => fs.closeSync(fd);
    } catch (err) {
      if (!BUSY.has(err.code)) throw err;
    }
    if (performance.now() >= deadline) throw new LockTimeoutError(lockPath, timeoutMs);
    sleepSync(wait + Math.random() * wait);
    wait = Math.min(wait * 1.5, 6);
  }
}

// Store: the replayed state plus the rules for reading and writing the log.
// Every read of shared state and every write happens inside withLock(), which
// first brings this process up to date with whatever other processes wrote.

import path from 'node:path';
import { compactLog } from './compactor.mjs';
import { QueueClosedError } from './errors.mjs';
import { FORMAT_VERSION, HEADER_SIZE } from './frame.mjs';
import { createFileOps, ensureDir } from './fsops.mjs';
import { acquireLock } from './lock.mjs';
import { createLogger } from './logger.mjs';
import { applyRecord, createState } from './state.mjs';
import { Wal } from './wal.mjs';

export const WAL_FILE = 'wal.log';
export const LOCK_FILE = 'queue.lock';

const log = createLogger('store');

export class Store {
  constructor(dir, config) {
    this.dir = dir;
    this.config = config;
    this.clock = config.clock;
    this.ops = createFileOps();
    this.wal = new Wal(path.join(dir, WAL_FILE), this.ops);
    this.lockPath = path.join(dir, LOCK_FILE);
    this.state = createState();
    this.generation = null;
    this.offset = HEADER_SIZE;
    this.snapshotBytes = 0;
    this.tornTailsDropped = 0;
    this.lastCompactionError = null;
    this.closed = false;
    this.holdingLock = false;
  }

  open() {
    ensureDir(this.dir);
    this.#locked(() => {
      if (!this.wal.exists()) this.wal.create();
      this.catchUp();
    });
  }

  close() {
    this.closed = true;
  }

  #locked(fn) {
    if (this.holdingLock) throw new Error('queue lock is not reentrant');
    const release = acquireLock(this.lockPath, { timeoutMs: this.config.lockTimeoutMs });
    this.holdingLock = true;
    try {
      return fn();
    } finally {
      this.holdingLock = false;
      release();
    }
  }

  withLock(fn) {
    if (this.closed) throw new QueueClosedError();
    return this.#locked(() => {
      this.catchUp();
      return fn();
    });
  }

  /** Brings this.state up to date with the log without doing anything else. */
  refresh() {
    this.withLock(() => {});
  }

  catchUp() {
    const header = this.wal.readHeader();
    if (this.generation === null || this.ops.size(this.wal.file) < this.offset) {
      this.state = createState();
      this.offset = HEADER_SIZE;
      this.generation = header.generation;
    }

    const { frames, end, tail } = this.wal.scanFrom(this.offset);
    for (const frame of frames) {
      applyRecord(this.state, frame.record, this.clock);
      if (frame.record.t === 'snapshot') this.snapshotBytes = frame.size;
    }
    this.offset = end;

    if (tail) {
      this.tornTailsDropped += 1;
      log.warn('ignored incomplete tail', { offset: end, reason: tail.reason, bytes: tail.bytes });
    }
  }

  /** Must be called inside withLock(). */
  append(records, options) {
    this.offset = this.wal.append(records, options);
    for (const record of records) applyRecord(this.state, record, this.clock);
  }

  /** Must be called inside withLock(). */
  compact() {
    const bytesBefore = this.offset;
    const { snapshot, generation, size } = compactLog({
      wal: this.wal,
      ops: this.ops,
      state: this.state,
      generation: this.generation,
      now: this.clock.now(),
      config: this.config,
    });
    this.state = createState();
    applyRecord(this.state, snapshot);
    this.generation = generation;
    this.offset = size;
    this.snapshotBytes = size - HEADER_SIZE;
    log.info('compacted', { generation, bytesBefore, bytesAfter: size });
    return { generation, bytesBefore, bytesAfter: size, jobs: this.state.jobs.size };
  }

  /**
   * Compacts when the log has outgrown the threshold. A failure here must not
   * fail the operation that triggered it, because that operation has committed.
   */
  maybeCompact() {
    const threshold = this.config.compactAfterBytes;
    if (!threshold) return;
    if (this.offset < threshold) return;
    try {
      this.compact();
      this.lastCompactionError = null;
    } catch (err) {
      this.lastCompactionError = err;
      log.warn('compaction failed', { error: err.message });
    }
  }

  describe() {
    return {
      format: FORMAT_VERSION,
      generation: this.generation,
      logBytes: this.offset,
      tornTailsDropped: this.tornTailsDropped,
      lastCompactionError: this.lastCompactionError?.message ?? null,
    };
  }
}
// The queue's state is a pure fold over the log. Replaying the same records must
// give the same state in every process, at any time.

import { loadSnapshot } from './snapshot.mjs';

export function createState() {
  return { jobs: new Map(), idem: new Map(), seq: 0, completionSeq: 0 };
}

function requeue(job, availableAt, error) {
  job.status = 'ready';
  job.availableAt = availableAt;
  job.lease = null;
  job.lastError = error ?? job.lastError;
}

export function applyRecord(state, rec, clock) {
  if (rec.t === 'snapshot') {
    loadSnapshot(state, rec);
    return;
  }
  if (rec.t === 'enqueue') {
    state.seq += 1;
    state.jobs.set(rec.id, {
      id: rec.id,
      type: rec.type,
      payload: rec.payload,
      key: rec.key ?? null,
      priority: rec.priority ?? 0,
      status: 'ready',
      attempts: 0,
      fence: 0,
      maxAttempts: rec.maxAttempts,
      availableAt: rec.availableAt,
      lease: null,
      result: null,
      lastError: null,
      enqueuedAt: rec.at,
      completedAt: null,
      completionSeq: null,
      seq: state.seq,
    });
    if (rec.key) state.idem.set(rec.key, { id: rec.id, at: rec.at });
    return;
  }

  const job = state.jobs.get(rec.id);
  if (!job) return;

  switch (rec.t) {
    case 'claim':
      job.status = 'leased';
      job.attempts += 1;
      job.fence = rec.fence;
      job.lease = { worker: rec.worker, fence: rec.fence, until: rec.leaseUntil };
      break;
